WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.460
Tech enjoys a special immunity from liability.

00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:05.540
We should consider amending section 230.

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:11.240
For the privilege of 230 there has to be a bigger sense of responsibility.

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:15.280
Facebook, Twitter, and YouTube aren't open platforms, they're censors.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:19.580
TV personalities and politicians are calling for changes to section 230.

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:23.300
The 1996 law that makes possible the internet as we know it.

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:27.600
But they don't seem to have a clue about what the law actually says, or who it really protects.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:31.120
The goal here Kim is not to get less speech, the goal is to get more speech.

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:34.940
Section 230 allows for the free exchange of ideas on the internet.

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:38.960
And it may be just as important to online free speech as the First Amendment.

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:45.040
Section 230's most important sentence reads as follows:

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:50.020
No provider or user of an interactive computer service shall be treated as the publisher or speaker

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:53.900
of any information provided by another information content provider.

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:57.940
But a lot of people have found creative ways of redefining what the law says.

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:02.440
Big tech made effectively a bargain with Congress and a bargain with the American people.

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:04.800
We'll be neutral, we'll be fair.

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:11.880
We won't be biased, and in exchange for that, we'll receive what is effectively a federal subsidy.

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:14.760
Neutrality is not a condition of the law.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:19.300
In fact section 230 was designed in part so that internet companies could discriminate

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:23.960
by filtering out content that's illegal, indecent, or just otherwise objectionable.

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:29.900
Before section 230, online companies feared that any moderation would make them legally liable for user content.

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:33.120
Section 230 explicitly says that's not the case.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:38.160
Good faith and voluntary attempts to filter out unwanted posts and users are okay.

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:42.900
Without section 230 it would be hard for companies to avoid lawsuits and criminal charges

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:47.400
without either becoming cesspools of totally unmoderated speech,

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:50.520
or banning user-generated speech entirely.

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:52.840
They're becoming de-facto publishers.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:55.160
They're actually the messengers.

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:57.860
They're not the moderators of an open forum.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:03.240
There is no legal distinction in section 230 between a publisher and a platform.

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:05.000
The word platform doesn't even appear.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:10.860
What matters for legal purposes is who is responsible for creating particular web content.

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:16.620
Judgment calls about user speech, however poorly executed and whatever ideological biases are apparent,

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:20.840
just don't affect whether a company is broadly protected by section 230 or not.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:24.760
Big tech has gotten these giveaways from government, this sweetheart deal from government,

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:26.100
where they can't be held accountable.

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:29.380
Big tech enjoys a special immunity from liability.

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:32.860
People like to pretend section 230 created a legal loophole.

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:36.820
But the Congress that passed section 230 back in 1996 was explicit.

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:40.540
Section 230 would not apply when it comes to federal criminal laws.

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:43.000
And it would not apply to intellectual property law,

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:48.740
which means that copyright violators and serious criminals do not get a free pass because of section 230.

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:52.200
What the law does provide is limited protection from criminal charges

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:56.940
brought by state or local law authorities and some immunity from getting sued in civil court.

00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:00.640
This immunity is lost if a company creates illegal content itself

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:04.560
or if it edits content in a way that contributes to its illegality.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:09.160
And it's lost if a company participates in illegal acts to obtain that content,

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:13.360
or otherwise engages in or profits directly from some elicit action.

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:19.200
In general, section 230 is meant to leave room for holding online operators accountable for their own sins,

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.240
but not for those of their customers.

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:27.160
If they're gonna have this special immunity, that nobody else gets by the way, they should not discriminate.

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:29.720
Section 230 doesn't only benefit companies.

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:33.860
As attorney Jeff Kossef, author of The 26 Words That Created the Internet, says,

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:37.040
there are also significant free speech benefits to the public.

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:42.760
Section 230 shields not just providers of digital services from litigation, but the users of these services too.

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:47.740
Without it anyone could find themselves liable for retweeting, reblogging, or posting links to content

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:49.860
that is later found to break the law.

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:55.340
Yet for all the protections it provides to readers, writers, academics, shit posters, entrepreneurs, activists,

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:58.800
and amateur political pundits of every persuasion,

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:02.620
section 230 has somehow become a political pariah.

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:08.600
The political class wants everyone to believe that the way the US has policed the internet for the past quarter century,

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:11.960
has actually been lax, immoral, and dangerous.

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:18.280
Don't believe them. The future of free speech, and a lot more may depend on preserving section 230.

